# A copy of an enum's payload - a match binding, or "y := e as E.A" - is taken to hold its references in its own block,
# where they live where the enum's do. Passed on by value (build(y)), lent to a callee that builds through it (grow(y)),
# or a reference read out of it and captured (h := y.head), it has the callee build in the copy's block - a match clause,
# an inner block - and hang the new node off the enum's, which outlives that block: a use after free.
#     expected: "42 43 44" (as -i prints), or errors at the three calls
#     actual:   -b and -d print "-7 -7 -7" (the nodes' chunks reused by churn); built -d -s it stops at the first read:
#               "use after scope closed: storage a closed scope gave back was read or written"
# Found by the scope fuzzer (fuzz scope, B2f; 14 of its 62 findings over 300 programs). The same shapes for an enum the
# function was handed are /home/user/review/tonight3/repro/02d; a store written directly through the copy
# ("y.head.next = Node(45)") is built in the right place, and "y := w.b" (a field's copy) is refused by O17a.
# Cause (semantic.c): copyRefsHome (~15420, O25h) returns at once when its source is not an lvalue, and OperandIsLvalue
# (~8150) knows only READ_VAR, INDEX and MEMBER - not OPERATION_AS, which both "as" and a match binding's lowering read
# the payload through - so the copy's refsHome is never set; valueRefsHome (~7604) likewise walks down through MEMBER
# only. With no home recorded, valueRefsScope answers the copy's own storage: O4b binds build's scope variable there,
# O17a's splitValueHome sees no split (so grow(y) is not refused), and "h := y.head" takes the copy's block (O25a).
type Node struct(v I64) {
    v
    next mut Node& = null
}
type Box struct() { head mut Node& = Node(1) }
type E enum {
    A(b Box)
    Z
}
fn churn() I64 {
    t I64 = 0
    for i in range 200 {
        a := Array<I64>(64, -7)
        t += a[3]
    }
    return t
}
fn build(b Box) { b.head.next = Node(42) }
fn grow(b mut Box&) { b.head.next = Node(43) }
fn second(e E) I64 {
    b := e as E.A
    if b.head.next == null { return 0 }
    return b.head.next.v
}
fn main() ? {
    e1 := E.A(Box())
    match e1 {
        case E.A(y) { build(y) }
        case _ { }
    }
    e2 := E.A(Box())
    {
        y := e2 as E.A
        grow(y)
    }
    e3 := E.A(Box())
    match e3 {
        case E.A(y) {
            h := y.head
            f := fn(v I64) { h.next = Node(v) }
            f(44)
        }
        case _ { }
    }
    churn()
    println($second(e1) " " $second(e2) " " $second(e3))
}
